  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 504 AM EDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nAn upper level ridge/sfc high pressure builds in today and lingers \nthrough Wed. Because of this, we have a nice break in active weather \nthrough Wednesday, with partly to mostly clear skies, light winds, \nand highs in the 80s. Patchy fog is possible early this morning, but \nshould improve shortly after sunrise. \n\nNext chance for showers and storms looks to be Wed evening, \ncontinuing through early next week. Our upper level ridge drifts \neastward and a broad trough stemming from a closed low over Hudson \nBay takes hold of the Great Lakes region. A cold front will move in \nfrom NW to SE, stalling along the southern border of our CWA by \nSaturday morning. We'll see return flow around high pressure over \nthe Atlantic bring moisture from the gulf into the area during this \ntime. Have around a 40-80 percent chance for showers and storms Wed \nPM into Friday, which is when chances begin to dwindle from NW to \nSE. Heavy rain is possible. Severe weather is not expected at this \ntime. With the front stalling south of the CWA, we could end up dry \nSaturday outside of low 20 percent chances or so in the vicinity of \nthe front. Some of the guidance generates precipitation in the \nafternoon with an incoming shortwave, but my confidence isn't too \nhigh in this. Highs will be in the upper 70s and 80s.\n\nA couple additional waves move through the slightly troughed/zonal \npattern aloft Sunday into Tuesday, bringing chances for additional \nshowers/storms (20-40 percent). The best chances are associated with \nthe stronger wave on Monday into Tuesday. Highs will be in the\n80s.",
